  "Items the user puts in order by dragging them, with the pointer or a finger, or with the keys; lists of one group take each other’s items. A drop moves the element and tells you the indexes: your data stays yours.": "",
  "Children as items": "",
  "The JSX children are the items. Drag one, or focus it and press Ctrl + Down; `onReorder` says from where to where, and the app reorders its own data.": "",
  "Items from data, across lists": "",
  "`items` with an `itemTemplate` draws the rows from your data and keeps each row as the order changes. Two sortables of one `group` take each other’s items: `onRemove` and `onAdd` tell both sides, and each list draws its array again.": "",
  "A handle, side by side, a refused drag": "",
  "With the pointer after a few pixels; with a finger after a long press (a finger that moves before that scrolls). A copy follows the pointer, the item stays faint in its place, a line shows where it would land; the list scrolls near its edges.": "",
  "The item’s element moves there at once and `onReorder` says from where to where: your data is yours to reorder. Across sortables of a group, `onRemove` and `onAdd` say the same on both sides.": "",
  "With `items`, each item keeps its component as the order changes (the rows are keyed by item): reordering your array moves the rows, no row is made again.": "",
  "The focused item (its handle when there is one) takes Ctrl + Up / Down (Left / Right side by side), Ctrl + Home / End; each move is told to `onReorder` and read out.": "",
  "Cancels a drag; nothing moves.": "",
  "A `group` named by `label`; the items are whatever you put in it.": "",
  "One Tab stop that moves between the items with the keys; an item with a focusable part of its own keeps that. A handle is a button named by you.": "",
  "Each move by the keys is read out: “Moved to 2 of 5”.": "",
